  "id": "Compound::DB00563 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Methotrexate, a folic acid antagonist, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) through a complex network of interactions. The evidence suggests that Methotrexate may influence RA through the indirect pathway involving hydroxides. Specifically, Methotrexate is linked to Aseptic Necrosis and Skin Depigmentation, both of which are mediated by Hydrocortisone or Betamethasone. These corticosteroids are known to treat RA, indicating a potential mechanistic link between Methotrexate and RA via the hydroxide pathway. The low scores in the paths suggest that while there is a partial association, the evidence is not strong enough to definitively establish a causal relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of hydroxides in mediating the effects of Methotrexate on RA, though further research is needed to confirm the direct mechanism and to assess the clinical relevance of this relationship.",
